# Should you spend a bonus and a raise differently?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does Friedman's permanent income hypothesis say? How does Modigliani's life cycle fit with it?"
next: {"title":"How much should you change your spending when interest rates rise?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/82-how-much-should-you-change-your-spending.cast"}

## Two envelopes
sources:
    - id: keynes1936
      title: The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money
      authors: J. M. Keynes
      year: 1936
      finding: 'The ''fundamental psychological law'': consumption rises with income, but by less than the rise in income; a larger proportion of income is saved as real income rises.'
    - id: kuznets1946
      title: National Product since 1869
      authors: S. Kuznets
      year: 1946
      finding: 'Long-run US estimates from 1869: the ratio of consumption (and saving) to national income stayed roughly constant from decade to decade despite large growth in income.'

    icon jonas size 140 set twemoji x 380 y 585 or ["factory worker","construction worker"] icon_look picture of "man factory worker" hidden true
    text lab_jonas "Jonas: $50,000 a year" x 620 y 585 font_size 34 hidden true
    box env_bonus "Bonus\n$5,000 once" x 250 y 400 width 410 height 190 radius 12 icon envelope font_size 42 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    box env_raise "Raise\n$5,000 a year" x 750 y 400 width 410 height 190 radius 12 icon envelope font_size 42 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    text q_bonus "spent this year: ?" #8f887c x 250 y 255 font_size 34 hidden true
    text eq "=" #3d3833 x 500 y 400 font_size 80 hidden true
    text neq "≠ ?" #b5482e x 500 y 400 font_size 52 hidden true
    text q_raise "spent this year: ?" #8f887c x 750 y 255 font_size 34 hidden true
    text v_bonus "spent this year: $4,000" #b5482e x 250 y 255 font_size 34 hidden true
    text v_raise "spent this year: $4,000" #2f6b8f x 750 y 255 font_size 34 hidden true
    text keynes_rule "Keynes 1936: spend ~80¢ of each extra $" cites ["keynes1936"] x 500 y 175 font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch kz cites ["kuznets1946"] work ["Keynes: richer → save a bigger share","Kuznets, US 1869–1938: share saved ≈ flat"] x 500 y 600 font_size 32 hidden true
    mark kz_strike around kz_line_1 kind strike hidden true

    card title "Should you spend a bonus and a raise differently?"

Should you spend a bonus and a raise differently?
    draw env_bonus env_raise parallel true

Take Jonas, a welder at Harbourtown's boatyard, earning, say, fifty thousand dollars a year.
    draw jonas lab_jonas

His boss offers him a choice: a one (@highlight env_bonus@)-off bonus of five thousand dollars, or a raise of  (@highlight env_raise@)five thousand dollars a year.

B: Money is mone (@draw eq@)y. Five thousand dollars s (@draw q_bonus q_raise parallel true@)pends the same either way.

That was close to the standard view in the 1930s. John Maynar (@draw keynes_rule@)d Keynes argued that people spend a share of each extra dollar of income: say, eighty cents.

On that rule, Jonas spe (@hide q_bonus q_raise@)nds  (@draw v_bonus v_raise parallel true@)four thousand more this year, bonus or raise alike.

    pause 0.3

    quiz "Jonas takes the bonus. How much more does he spend this year?" judge false voice b
        right: Hold on to your guess.
        * About $4,000, as with a raise
        * Nearly all of it
        * Only a small part

(@hide v_bonus v_raise keynes_rule q_bonus q_raise jonas lab_jonas@) Keynes also expected people, and  (@draw kz_box kz_line_1@)whole countries, to save a bigger share of their income as they grew richer.

In 1946 Simon Kuznets checked, with American (@draw kz_line_2@) figures reaching back to 1869. Income had grown several times over, yet the share people saved barely m (@draw kz_strike@)oved, decade after decade.

    pause 0.4

B: So people (@erase kz kz_strike@) aren't simply spe (@show jonas lab_jonas@)nding a slice of this year (@hide eq@)'s (@draw neq@) pay. Then what  (@highlight neq@)are t (@show q_bonus q_raise@)hey spending?

## Permanent income
domain: {"x":[28,72],"y":[38,65]}
vars:
    d:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 10
      step: 1
      decimals: 0
      color: '#2f6b8f'
    yrs:
      value: 1
      min: 1
      max: 40
      step: 1
      decimals: 0
      color: '#2f6b8f'
    dcd:
      expr: 1000*d*yrs/40
sources:
    - id: friedman1957
      title: A Theory of the Consumption Function
      authors: M. Friedman
      year: 1957
      finding: Consumption depends on permanent income (expected average income), not on measured current income; transitory income is largely saved. Explains why cross-section saving rises with income while the long-run saving share is stable.

    axes ax x_label age y_label "$ thousand a year" cross corner ticks.x [30,40,50,60] ticks.y [40,50,60] hidden true
    curve spend0 dashed #8f887c expr "50" x_from 30 x_to 70 style.opacity 0.6 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    curve spend #b5482e expr "50 + d*yrs/40" x_from 30 x_to 70 style.opacity 0.8 style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    curve income thick #2f6b8f expr "50 + d*min(1, max(0, (x-29.9)*25))*min(1, max(0, (30.15+yrs-x)*25))" x_from 30 x_to 70 hidden true
    curve wobble dashed #2f6b8f expr "50 + 3.2*sin(1.9*(x-30)) + 1.8*sin(0.7*(x-30)+1)" x_from 30 x_to 70 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    label l_income "income" above #2f6b8f attach_to income font_size 32 hidden true
    label l_spend "spending" below #b5482e attach_to spend font_size 32 hidden true
    label l_perm "permanent" below #3d3833 attach_to income font_size 32 hidden true
    text l_trans "transitory" #2f6b8f at.data [49,57.8] font_size 32 hidden true
    text readout "extra spending: ${dcd:0,} a year" #b5482e at.data [61,62.5] font_size 32 hidden true
    portrait friedman cites ["friedman1957"] x 880 y 520 width 170 of "Milton Friedman" cameo true hidden true
    text smooth_note "consumption smoothing" #b5482e at.data [50,43] font_size 34 hidden true
    math ctl #2f6b8f at.data [38,62.5] tex "\\text{extra pay: }\\${d}\\text{k} \\times {yrs}\\text{ years}" size 34 hidden true

    card title "Permanent income"

B: Milton Friedman's answer came in 1957: people spend from what they expect to earn over the years ahead, not from this year's pay.
    draw friedman

B: (@erase friedman@) Here's Jonas's income  (@draw ax income l_income@)from age thirty, say: fifty thousand dollars a year, for forty working years. Leave interest and retirement aside for now.

Real paychecks  (@draw wobble@)wobble, though. Overtime one year, a slow season the next.

B: Friedman split income in two. The steady level  (@draw l_perm@)he called permanent income; the wobble, tr (@draw l_trans@)ansitory income.

B: Spending, he said, f (@erase l_perm@)ollows th (@draw spend l_spend@)e permanent part: fifty thousand a year, smooth, whatever the wobble.

Why s (@highlight spend@)mooth? Why not splash out in the good years?

B: Because the tenth dinner out in a month gives you less than the first. Saving and borrowing let you (@erase wobble l_trans@) move money between years, so you spread it evenly. That's cons (@draw smooth_note@)umption smoothing.

(@erase smooth_note@) So  (@draw ctl@)here com (@draw spend0@)es th (@animate d 5 duration 1.5@)e bonus: five thousand dollars, once.

B: In the idea's cleanest form, spread over a whole forty-year horizon, that's a (@draw ctl readout@) hundred and twenty-five dollars more a year. The spending line barely moves.

And the raise is  (@animate yrs 40 duration 3.5 easing linear@)the same five thousand, every year for forty years. Spending rises by the full five thousand.

    pause 0.4

Try it: set how long the extra money lasts, from one year to forty, and watch how much of it Jonas spends now.
    explore

B: And that cracks Kuznets's puzzle. As a country grows richer (@highlight spend@), permanent income grows too, and spending keeps pace, so the share saved needn't climb.

## The life cycle
domain: {"x":[28,82],"y":[0,72]}
vars:
    k:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 1
    d:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 10
      step: 1
      decimals: 0
    sp:
      expr: 800*d
    sv:
      expr: 200*d
sources:
    - id: modbrum1954
      title: 'Utility analysis and the consumption function: an interpretation of cross-section data'
      authors: F. Modigliani; R. Brumberg
      year: 1954
      finding: 'The life-cycle hypothesis: people plan consumption over their whole lifetime, saving while working and running wealth down in retirement; aggregate saving depends on income growth and age structure.'
    - id: carroll2001
      title: A Theory of the Consumption Function, With and Without Liquidity Constraints
      authors: C. D. Carroll
      year: 2001
      finding: Recounts that Friedman took the horizon of permanent income to be about three years and asserted (1963) a marginal propensity to consume out of transitory income of about one third.

    axes ax x_label age y_label "$ thousand a year" cross corner ticks.x [30,40,50,60,70,80] ticks.y [0,20,40,60] hidden true
    curve income thick #2f6b8f expr "(50 + d)*min(1, max(0, (70.05 - x)*40))" x_from 30 x_to 80 hidden true
    curve spend #b5482e expr "(50 + d)*40/(40 + 10*k)" x_from 30 x_to 80 style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    curve spend40 dashed #8f887c expr "40" x_from 30 x_to 80 style.opacity 0.6 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    region save_reg x_from 30 x_to 69.9 between ["income","spend"] style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    region dis_reg x_from 70.2 x_to 80 between ["spend","income"] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.4 hidden true
    label l_income "income" above #2f6b8f attach_to income hidden true
    text l_spend "spending" #b5482e at.data [76,52] font_size 30 hidden true
    text l_save "saving" #a4781c at.data [50,37] font_size 32 hidden true
    text l_dis "living off savings" #b5482e at.data [75,20] font_size 28 hidden true
    text l_peak "savings peak" #3d3833 at.data [70,64] font_size 30 hidden true
    portrait modigliani cites ["modbrum1954"] x 880 y 640 width 170 of "Franco Modigliani" cameo true hidden true
    scale guess x 180 y 230 width 520 label "extra spending a year, $" min 0 max 5000 value 4000 ticks 5 hidden true
    text readout "spends +${sp:0,} · saves +${sv:0,}" #b5482e at.data [50,64] font_size 32 hidden true
    text bonus_note "bonus: +$100 a year" #b5482e at.data [48,25] font_size 32 hidden true
    text c_mod "Modigliani: a whole life" #3d3833 at.data [48,27] cites ["modbrum1954"] font_size 32 hidden true
    text c_fri "Friedman: ~3 years ahead" #3d3833 at.data [48,17] cites ["carroll2001"] font_size 32 hidden true
    text nation "a country's saving: growth + age" #3d3833 at.data [48,22] font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch mathcard work ["C = 40 × $50k ÷ 50 years = $40k","extra C = extra pay × years it lasts ÷ 50","bonus: $5,000 × 1 ÷ 50 = $100","raise: $5,000 × 40 ÷ 50 = $4,000"] x 420 y 215 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "The life cycle"

(@draw ax income l_income@) Friedman's smoothing runs on a paycheck that never stops. In 1954 Franco (@draw modigliani@) Modigliani and his student Richard Brumberg built in the obvious catch: one day it does.

Say Jonas works from thirty,  (@highlight income@)retires at seventy and lives to eighty. Ten years with no paycheck.

(@erase modigliani@) Forty yea (@draw spend l_spend@)rs of earnings now have to last (@animate k 1 duration 2.5@) fifty years, so he spends forty thousand a year.

B: And the gap wh (@draw save_reg l_save@)ile he works is saving, which he (@draw dis_reg l_dis@) lives off once he stops.

So his savings climb to a peak at  (@draw l_peak@)retirement, then run down. Modigliani called it the life cycle.

B: Then (@fade ax income spend save_reg dis_reg l_income l_spend l_save l_dis l_peak to 0.15@) the raise mu (@draw guess@)st look different now too.

    ask "With retirement in the picture, how much of a $5,000 raise does Jonas spend each year? Click on the line." on guess store g default "2500" stage own voice b
        right: Right: about four thousand.
        wrong: Remember: the raise lasts forty years, but it has to cover fifty: here, about $4,000.

(@erase guess l_peak@) Fo (@fade ax income spend save_reg dis_reg l_income l_spend l_save l_dis to 1@)ur t (@draw spend40@)housand: fo (@animate d 5 duration 2@)rty years of raises, spread o (@draw readout@)ver fifty years of life. The other thousand goes towards retirement.

B: And the bonus s (@draw bonus_note@)hrinks to a hundred dollars a year.

Same core a (@erase bonus_note readout@)s Friedman: spending fo (@highlight spend@)llows lifetime resources, not this year's pay. The two differed on the horizon.

B: Modiglia (@draw c_mod@)ni meant a whole life. Friedman himself thought people lo (@draw c_fri@)oked only about three years ahead, and would spend about a third of a windfall in its first year.

Modigliani drew (@erase c_mod c_fri@) a bold conclusion for whole countries: their saving depends less on how ri (@draw nation@)ch they are than on how fast they grow and how old they are.

B: Because workers save and re (@highlight l_save@)tirees spend down. A young, fast-grow (@highlight l_dis@)ing country has more savers than spenders.

    erase nation

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: The arithmetic behind those numbers is short.

@math

    erase mathq

With no interest, Jonas spends his lifetime earnings divided by his years of life: forty years of fifty thousand, over fifty years, is forty thousand.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1

Any extra pay adds its size, times the years it lasts, divided by the years of life.
    draw mathcard_line_2

A bonus lasts one year: five thousand over fifty, a hundred dollars.
    draw mathcard_line_3

A raise lasts forty: four thousand a year.
    draw mathcard_line_4

    erase mathcard

@after

    erase mathq

## Who spends it down?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Kotlikoff & Summers 1981
      - Modigliani 1988
    values:
      - 80
      - 20
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_label: US wealth inherited
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 150
      w: 560
      h: 470
    label_size: 24
sources:
    - id: ks1981
      title: The Role of Intergenerational Transfers in Aggregate Capital Accumulation
      authors: L. J. Kotlikoff; L. H. Summers
      year: 1981
      finding: Estimated that intergenerational transfers (inheritances and gifts) account for roughly 80% of US wealth; life-cycle saving for only about a fifth.
    - id: mod1988
      title: The Role of Intergenerational Transfers and Life Cycle Saving in the Accumulation of Wealth
      authors: F. Modigliani
      year: 1988
      finding: Argued that transfers account for roughly a fifth of US wealth; the gap with Kotlikoff–Summers comes largely from definitions, such as whether interest on inherited wealth and parents' payments for college count as transfers.

    icon old size 110 set twemoji x 760 y 540 or ["older woman","old man"] icon_look picture of "old woman" hidden true
    icon house size 110 set twemoji x 900 y 540 icon_look picture of house hidden true
    text q_spend "spend it all?" x 830 y 430 font_size 32 hidden true
    text reasons "cushion · care · heirs" #8f887c x 830 y 360 font_size 30 hidden true
    text defs "what counts as inherited?" #8f887c cites ["mod1988"] x 830 y 280 font_size 28 hidden true
    text st_agreed "mostly agreed" #4f7a3a x 830 y 540 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c_agreed "raise moves spending more" x 830 y 485 font_size 30 hidden true
    text st_contested "contested" #b5482e x 830 y 360 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c_contested "how much we smooth, and why" x 830 y 305 font_size 30 hidden true
    box ans_bonus "Bonus\nspread over a life" x 250 y 400 width 410 height 190 radius 12 icon envelope font_size 40 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    box ans_raise "Raise\nspend most of it" x 750 y 400 width 410 height 190 radius 12 icon envelope font_size 40 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    text next_q "and if the interest rate r moves?" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 220 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Who spends it down?"

(@draw old q_spend@) The life cycle has old people running their savings down to nothing. Do they?

B: My grandmother didn't. She di (@draw house@)ed in her own house, with money in the bank.

She's typical. Many people run their wealth down far more slowly than the model says: some (@draw reasons@) keep a cushion for illness or care, many want to leave something behind.

In 1981 Laurence Kotlikoff and Lawrence Summers put a num (@draw axes bar_1@)ber on it: roughly four-fifths of American wealth, they estimated, came from inheritances and gifts, not from saving for retirement.

B: Modigliani pushed back h (@draw bar_2@)ard: by his count, closer to a fifth.

Much of the gap is definitions:  (@draw defs@)does the interest on an inheritance count as inherited, or the college fees your parents paid?

(@erase old house q_spend reasons defs@) How sure are we? That a lasting raise moves spending (@draw st_agreed c_agreed@) more than a one-off bonus: mostly agreed.

B: (@erase axes bar_1 bar_2@) How much people really smooth, and  (@draw st_contested c_contested@)why they hold on to wealth: contested. The evidence deserves a lecture of its own.

    quiz "A Harbourtown fisher has a record season, but expects normal catches from next year on. What does the permanent income idea predict?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: She saves most of it: one good season barely changes what she expects to earn over the years.
        wrong: Ask whether one good season changes what she expects to earn over the years: here, she saves most of it.
        * She spends most of the windfall this year
        + She saves most of it
        * She borrows to spend even more

    quiz "A medical student earns little now but expects a high salary later. What does the life-cycle model predict for her spending today?" voice b
        right: Above her income: she spends from her lifetime resources and borrows against her future pay.
        wrong: Spending follows lifetime resources, not this year's pay: here, above her income.
        * Below her small income, saving hard
        * About equal to her income
        + Above her income, borrowing

    quiz "Two Harbourtown neighbours, one aged 30 and one aged 75, each win $100,000 in the lottery. On the life-cycle model, who spends more of it this year?"
        right: The 75-year-old: she spreads the win over far fewer years left.
        wrong: Divide the win by the years each has left to spend it: here, the 75-year-old.
        * The 30-year-old
        + The 75-year-old
        * Both the same: it's the same win

So, should yo (@erase c_agreed c_contested st_agreed st_contested@)u spend a bonus and a raise differentl (@draw ans_bonus ans_raise parallel true@)y? Yes: a raise changes your whole life's budget, a bonus changes one year's.

B: We kept the interest rate fixed (@draw next_q@). How hard should a saver react when it moves? That's the next question.

